onsistent with the lattice QCD calculations [65, 66] in the sensitive temperature range, corresponding to \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\,\)GeV (between \(\sim\)1-2 \(T_{\rm c}\), where \(T_{\rm c}\) is the temperature of the critical point).

### 2.5 Measurement of the \(\overline{\rm D^{0}}/\rm D^{0}\) ratio

So far, we have discussed combined measurements of both \(\rm D^{0}\) and its antiparticle \(\overline{\rm D^{0}}\). The charm quarks are produced in pairs, therefore, we would expect the same number of \(\rm D^{0}\) and \(\overline{\rm D^{0}}\). The Statistical-Hadronization Model [107], however, suggests that the \(\overline{\Lambda_{\rm c}}^{-}/\Lambda_{\rm c}^{+}\) is lower than unity and the c-quarks can get, therefore, depleted, compared to \(\rm\overline{c}\)-quarks. Figure 2.10shows the \(\overline{\rm D^{0}}\)/\(\rm D^{0}\) invariant-yields ratio plotted versus \(p_{\rm T}\), together with constant function fits of the data [90]. Although the ratio is at 1 when divided into \(p_{\rm T}\) bins, the overall ratio is significantly larger than unity in the 0-60 % most central collisions. E.g. in the 0-10 % most central collisions, the fit is 4.9 \(\sigma\) higher than unity.

### 2.6 \(\rm D_{s}\) measurements

Thanks to the HFT, the \(\rm D_{s}\) meson, consisting of a charm quark and a strange quark, is measured for the first time at RHIC [108]. Such measurements can shed more light on the mechanism of the charm quark coalescence.

In Figure 2.11, the yield ratio of \(\rm D_{s}\)/\(\rm D^{0}\) measured by STAR is shown as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\)[108]. The \(\rm D^{0}\) spectrum is obtained from the published STAR data [90]. No significant change of the ratio with centrality is observed. Moreover, the measured \(\rm D_{s}\)/\(\rm D^{0}\) ratio is consistent within uncertainties with a similar measurement from minimum-bias Pb+Pb collisions measured by ALICE [109]. To compare our measurement to the \(\rm D_{s}\)/\(\rm D^{0}\) in p+p collisions, PYTHIA 6.4 [62] is used, tuned to \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV (green curve) and \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=7\) TeV (magenta curve). The PYTHIA simulations are consistent with the ALICE measurement [110] of the \(\rm D_{s}\)/\(\rm D^{0}\) ratio in p+p collisions (small brown circles). The STAR measurement is significantly enhanced compared to the p+p ratio in all the centrality brackets.

In Figure 2.12, we compare the \(\rm D_{s}\)/\(\rm D^{0}\) yield ratio, as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\), to several theoretical predictions that incorporate quark coalescence in their calculations. The Tsinghua model [112] stands out as the only one that includes sequential coalescence in which the \(\rm D_{s}\) hadronize earlier than \(\rm D^{0}\), denoted as Tsinghua (seq. coal.). This model does not count with any hadronization through fragmentation, only coalescence. The Catania model [113] can compare scenarios with only coalescence hadronization - Catania (coal.) - and with both, fragmentation and coalescence - Catania (coal.+frag.). The He, Rapp [114] calculation incorporates in itself resonance recombination model that conserves the number of heavy quarks, energy, and momentum. This model includes both, fragmentation and coalescence hadronization scenarios. The Cao,Ko model [115] also contains fragmentation and coalescence, and energy conservation.

In the \(p_{\rm T}>4\,\)GeV/\(c\) region, the measured \(\rm D_{s}/D^{0}\) yield ratio in 0-10 % most central collisions features the same general enhancement, compared to the PYTHIA p+p simulation, as models that include quark coalescence Tsinghua, Catania (coal.), He,Rapp, and Cao,Ko. There is, however, some tension in the lower-\(p_{\rm T}\) region. On the other hand, the Catania (coal.+frag.) model describes the data in the low-\(p_{\rm T}\) region, but fails to predict the high-\(p_{\rm T}\) data points. The Tsinghua calculation for 20-40 % centrality is close to the measured data as shown in Figure 2.12(b). Overall, this comparison shows that charm-quark coalescence in

