## Chapter 4 Reconstruction of the \(\Lambda_{\rm c}\) Baryon in Au+Au Collisions

\(\Lambda_{\rm c}^{\pm}\) is the lightest baryon containing a charm quark. As such, it is an excellent probe into the behavior of the QGP alongside precise measurements of D mesons. However, the combination of facts that it has an extremely short lifetime times the speed of light \(c\tau\sim 60\,\upmu\)m and the the useful decay for a direct reconstruction is a three-body decay into \(\uppi^{\pm}\), K\({}^{\mp}\), and p\({}^{\pm}\), makes this measurement challenging. \(\uppi^{\pm}\), K\({}^{\mp}\), and p\({}^{\pm}\) are the most abundant particles coming out of heavy-ion collisions which implies that any measurement of \(\Lambda_{\rm c}^{\pm}\) has to deal with a large background.

The \(\Lambda_{\rm c}^{\pm}\) were measured for the first time in heavy-ion-ion collisions [77] at the STAR experiment at RHIC. In particular, the \(\Lambda_{\rm c}\) measurement was enabled by the Heavy Flavor Tracker (HFT) [1] upgrade that took data in the years 2014-2016. The HFT is described in more detail in section 3.5. In this chapter, the measurement of the \(\Lambda_{\rm c}\) from signal extraction to the ratios of \(\Lambda_{\rm c}/{\rm D}^{0}\) are described. These results were published in J. Adam et al., _"First Measurement of \(\Lambda_{c}\) Baryon Production in Au+Au Collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\it NN}}=200\,\)GeV"_, Phys. Rev. Lett. **124**, 172301 (2020) [9]. As one of the primary authors, I will describe the entire analysis, published in this paper, in more detail than is possible within the scope of an an article in a scientific journal.

### 4.1 Event and track selection

In this analysis, we use data recorded by STAR from Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV taken in 2014 and 2016. The Au+Au collisions are selected so that the PV is valid and is reconstructed fully inside the volume of the HFT. The distance of the PV from the center of the TPC along the \(z\)-axis is required to be \(|v_{z}|<6\) cm and the difference between the \(v_{z}\) determined from the tracks and the one determined by the VPD has to be less than 3 cm. In addition, the distance from the center along the \(x,y\) plane \(v_{r}=\sqrt{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}}\) is required to be less than 2 cm.

For the \(\Lambda_{\rm c}\) analysis, we use triplets of pions, Kaons, and pions. The HFT tracking is required which means: In the 2014 data, the tracks are required to have at least one hit in each pixel layer and a hit in the IST (the SSD is not used in the tracking in 2014); in 2016 data, we require at least one hit in each pixel layer and at least one hit in either IST or SSD. In the TPC, tracks were selected to ensure that they are not fakes consisting of hits that do not come from one particle, but e.g. a combination of noise or hits from several tracks combined into one. We require the number of hits \(N_{\rm hits}\geq 20\) and the ratio to maximum number of hits in the track trajectory \(N_{\rm hits}/N_{\rm hits\;max}>0.52\). The minimum track \(p_{\rm T}\) was chosen as 0.5 GeV/\(c\) to save computation time and because bellow, the \(\Lambda_{\rm c}\) cannot be extracted as they are drowned in the combinatorial background.

The PID-selection criteria are summarized in Table 4.1. Looser cuts were applied to the \(\pi^{\pm}\), because of their higher relative abundance, compared to K\({}^{\mp}\) and p\({}^{\pm}\). In the TPC, we compare the d\(E\)/d\(x\) value of the track to the width of the distribution \(\sigma_{\rm d}E\)/d\(x\) of each particle species at the track momentum

\[N_{\sigma}=\frac{{\rm d}E/{\rm d}x}{\sigma_{\rm d}E/{\rm d}x}\,. \tag{4.1}\]

We set the cut at \(|N_{\sigma}|<3\) for \(\pi^{\pm}\) and \(|N_{\sigma}|<2\) for K\({}^{\mp}\) and p\({}^{\pm}\).

For the PID in TOF, we use the, so called, hybrid approach: The time of flight information is used if the track is matched to a TOF hit. The cut on the fraction \(\beta\) of the speed of light, compared to the supposed speed of the particle divided by \(c\) at its momentum \(p\) and supposedrest mass \(m\) of the respective particle species is set as

\[\left|\Delta\frac{1}{\beta}\right|=\left|\frac{1}{\beta}-\frac{1}{\beta_{\rm sup }}\right|=\left|\frac{1}{\beta}-\frac{p}{\sqrt{m^{2}c^{2}+p^{2}}}\ \right|<0.03 \tag{4.2}\]

for all particles. For protons and kaons, we use the hybrid approach for high-\(p_{\rm T}\) tracks and we require the TOF hit for tracks with \(p_{\rm T}<3\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\) and \(p_{\rm T}<2\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\), respectively. For pions, we use the hybrid approach throughout the whole \(p_{\rm T}\) spectrum. All tracks are then saved and combined into triplets so that additional cuts can be applied.

### 4.2 Centrality determination

The centrality of the collision is determined by the number of tracks \(N_{\rm tracks}\) with at least 10 TPC hits in the rapidity range \(|\eta|<0.5\) and DCA \(<3\,\mathrm{cm}\). This number is then corrected with regards to the geometrical efficiency according to \(v_{z}\) and to the TPC efficiency, according to instant luminosity. Figure 4.2 shows the centrality definitions extracted from Monte-Carlo simulations [172, 173], using the Glauber Model. This model was used to extract the number of binary nucleon-nucleon collisions \(N_{\rm coll}\) and the number of participants \(N_{\rm part}\) as well.

### 4.3 Topological reconstruction and cuts optimization

In the three-body \({\Lambda_{\rm c}}^{\pm}\) decay, all the three daughter particles come practically from the same secondary vertex (SV), even in the case of the decays through resonances, because they decay

