### 4.7 Efficiency correction

To calculate the real yield of the \(\Lambda_{\rm c}\) baryons in Au+Au collisions, the raw yields have to be corrected for efficiency of the detectors and of the used analysis method. We assume that the efficiencies of the various subdetectors and applied cuts are independent, therefore we can factorize this correction into several parts. The final efficiency \(\epsilon_{\Lambda_{\rm c}}\) is a product of the detection efficiency in the TPC \(\epsilon_{\rm TPC}\) and the HFT \(\epsilon_{\rm HFT}\), as well as the probability that the \(\Lambda_{\rm c}\) triplets passed the BDT cuts \(\epsilon_{\rm BDT}\) and the particle-identification cuts \(\epsilon_{\rm PID}\). The full correction can be written as

\[\epsilon_{\Lambda_{\rm c}}=\epsilon_{\rm TPC}\times\epsilon_{\rm PID}\times \epsilon_{\rm HFT}\times\epsilon_{\rm BDT}\times\Delta\epsilon_{\rm sec} \times\Delta\epsilon_{\rm vtx}\,, \tag{4.8}\]

where \(\Delta\epsilon_{\rm sec}\) denotes the correction for the secondary contribution to the \(\Lambda_{\rm c}\) yield and \(\Delta\epsilon_{\rm vtx}\) is the correction for the resolution of the primary vertex position.

#### TPC tracking efficiency

The acceptance and efficiency of tracking are evaluated, by embedding of Monte-Carlo (MC) generated p, K, and \(\pi\) tracks into measured Au+Au collision events in order to take into account the real occupancy and pileup in the data. The Au+Au collisions are randomly selected over the measured time period. The MC tracks are generated with a flat \(p_{\rm T}\), \(y\)and \(\phi\) distributions so that their number does not exceed \(5\,\%\) of the track multiplicity in the Au+Au event. The generated MC tracks are subsequently passed through the full GEANT [4] simulation of the STAR-detector geometry. The combined signal is then processed in the same way as in real data. The TPC tracking efficiency \(\epsilon_{\rm TPC}\)(daughter) is a ratio of the tracks that satisfy the track selection criteria \(N_{\rm rec}\) divided by all simulated tracks \(N_{\rm emb}\)

\[\epsilon_{\rm TPC}({\rm daughter})=\frac{N_{\rm rec}(|\eta|<1\cap N_{\rm hits} \geq 20\cap N_{\rm hits}/N_{\rm hits\ max}>0.52\cap{\rm DCA}<1.5\,{\rm cm})}{N_{ \rm emb}(|\eta|<1)}\,. \tag{4.9}\]

Because the efficiencies of detection of different tracks are independent, the final efficiency \(\epsilon_{\rm TPC}\) is calculated as a product of the daughter efficiencies

\[\epsilon_{\rm TPC}=\prod_{\rm daughter=p,\ K,\ \pi}\epsilon_{\rm TPC}({\rm daughter })\,. \tag{4.10}\]

Figures 4.20, 4.21, and 4.22 show the TPC tracking efficiency of p, K, and \(\pi\), respectively.

